### Normality

The following analysis will test for normality of the distribution of Alpha Diversity metrics, which will determine whether analyses should be performed using parametric or non-parametric tests. This table gives summary statistics for a variaty of tests related to the distribution of the data to helkp make this decision. However, generally speaking, if the p-value for the Shapiro-Wilk test is greater than 0.05, then one can assume that the assumption of normality will hold (i.e. there is not sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis).

```{r Section2_QC_filterdata_alpha_diversity_normality}
jmv::descriptives(
    formula = Observed + Chao1 + Shannon + Simpson ~ Group,
    data = adiv,
    iqr = TRUE,
    sw = TRUE)
```

### t-test

```{r Section2_QC_filterdata_alpha_diversity_comparison_stat}
jmv::ttestIS(
    formula = Shannon ~ Group,
    data = adiv,
    vars = vars(Shannon),
    eqv = TRUE,
    effectSize = TRUE)
```

For Shannon, Levene's test is violated (p < 0.05) so we will now use a Welch t-test: 

```{r Section2_QC_filterdata_alpha_diversity_comparison_stat_welch}
jmv::ttestIS(
    formula = Shannon ~ Group,
    data = adiv,
    vars = vars(Shannon),
    welchs = TRUE,
    students = FALSE,
    eqv = TRUE,
    effectSize = TRUE)
```


```{r Section2_QC_filterdata_alpha_diversity_comparison_stat_non_parametric}
jmv::ttestIS(
    formula = Observed + Chao1 + Simpson ~ Group,
    data = adiv,
    vars = vars(Observed, Chao1, Simpson),
    students = FALSE,
    mann = TRUE,
    effectSize = TRUE)
```

